  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:flp-impossibility",
  "domain": "distributed-systems",
  "definition": "The FLP Impossibility (Fischer, Lynch, Paterson 1985) is a foundational theorem in distributed computing proving that no deterministic protocol can solve the [[Consensus]] problem in a fully asynchronous message-passing system if even one process may crash. The result arises because message delays are unbounded, making it impossible to distinguish a crashed process from a slow one, so any protocol that always terminates can be manipulated into a state of permanent indecision. Consequently, every practical [[Consensus Algorithm]] must relax at least one of the FLP assumptions \u2014 typically by introducing partial synchrony, randomisation, or probabilistic termination.",
